 night owls  or or not and uh so yes for a lot of  people they will just naturally be night  owls they will be inclined to sleep very  late and wake up later and so for those  folks it's it's a problem because you're  you're starting school that early and  you will exclude these people in that  way you won't have their full uh you  know brain capacity uh you know you'd be  using that so so it is something but  i'll say something i you know in my  entire life david i was i was sleeping  maybe at two or three a.m that's just my  natural thing but the last four years  i've been sleeping at 10 p.m you know so  i've totally shifted how i sleep as well  and it just works better so sometimes  even though you think you're a night owl  by nature you might in fact be somebody  who's not so no me too i mean i i tried  i also saw this natural shift where  because of my schedule 10 15 and i was  just getting into bed and i thought to  myself i used to kind of be a night owl  let me see what happens if i try to just  stay up a little later and get up a  little later it felt very unnatural  where i was just fine it was a you know  11  and i was just like i i should just be  in bed right now this this doesn't feel  like it's my schedule any longer  100 that's what happened to me and i was  surprised and i was i thought i was  genetically just you know doomed to be  somebody who has you know sleep at 3 a.m  but yeah you know i sleep much earlier  and i feel better the the work in the  field is fascinating we've been speaking  with dr balan jalal who's a researcher  at harvard university and also a  visiting researcher at cambridge  university really a pleasure having you  on and i appreciate your time today  my pleasure david
 

